<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">İspir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ispir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">İspir is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the upper Çoruh river. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its castle was contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Turks; it was held by the Saltukids of Erzurum in the 12th century before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Its castle still stands above the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ispir.jpg" alt="İspir" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IspirCastle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>İspir is a municipality and district of Erzurum Province with an area of 2,129 square kilometres, on the Çoruh River. In Armenian it is Sper and in Georgian Speri. The place is known from the 3rd millennium BCE. It lay in the upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the Chorokh, where the kingdom of Hayasa-Azzi of the 2nd millennium BCE was located and is said to have included Sper. Some think the name Sper derives from Saspers or Sasperi, the name with a Georgian prefix of place, Sa-, which evolved into the term Iberian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Sper was organised as a province of the Iberian Kingdom in the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE. Alexander the Great is said to have sent his general Menon to conquer it, but Menon and his forces were defeated and killed. From the 2nd century BCE to the 5th century CE it belonged to Upper Armenia, a province of Greater Armenia, and in the 4th to 6th centuries it was an Armenian Bagratid domain whose territory also included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayburt">Bayburt</a> plain until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> took that, perhaps in 387. The Georgian account holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> originated in Speri.</p><p>In the 7th century the area passed to the Arab Caliphate, and in 885 to the Bagratuni Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Under the medieval kingdom it belonged to Upper Armenia and was known for its gold mines. The Seljuqs conquered it in the 11th century.</p><p>The Saltukids held it until 1124, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> took over and governed it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> under Zakare and Ivane Zakarids. Mughith ad Din Tughrul, son of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a>, recaptured it at some point between 1201 and 1225 and built a mosque in the citadel that still survives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered it in 1242. The Georgian Kingdom regained it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> the Brilliant (1314-1346), and later it passed to the Georgian atabegs of the House of Jaqeli. Persia conquered it in 1502, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> probably took it from the Georgian ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> in 1515. The Russians occupied the town in 1916, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>, and the Turks recaptured it in 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district had a population of 14,607 in 2022. Its historic sights include the citadel, with a mosque and a church probably of the 13th century, the Sultan Melik mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of the 13th century, and the Madrasa of Kadizade Mehmet, built in 1725/26. Kadizade was mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> from 1744 to 1759, and his father was qadi of İspir. The Çarşı mosque is originally of the 13th century, though its building is recent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “İspir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Saltukids held Erzurum, Tercan, Bayburt and <b>Ispir</b>, on the frontier with the Kingdom of Georgia, which defeated them several times; Izz al-Din Saltuk II was captured by the Georgians in the 1150s and ransomed.</blockquote><a href="/state/saltukids">Saltukids</a></li></ul></section>
